\section{Choosing Rematerialization over a Spill Home}

A next-use number says when an evicted value must return, but not
what it costs to bring it back. A local allocator can save a store
by remembering a recipe. Consider a scalar register file and a small
catalogue of one-instruction recipes, either an immediate constant
or a nontrapping register-plus-immediate computation. The recipe must
reproduce the same bits, must not change observable flags, and must
name operands guaranteed to be available at the eventual use. In this
restricted example any register operand of a recipe lives in a fixed,
unclobbered register. This excludes recursive regeneration and avoids
assuming a temporary will still be available after eviction.

\begin{sudocode}[Eviction and restoration for one value]
\Function{Evict}{$v,r$}
  \If{$v$ has no future use or exit requirement}
    \State \Return \textsc{Dead}
  \ElsIf{$v$ has a current stack copy}
    \State \Return its slot \Comment{No duplicate store}
  \ElsIf{\Call{ValidRecipe}{$v$}}
    \State \Return recipe for $v$ \Comment{Exact bits, no effects}
  \EndIf
  \State $h\gets\Call{FreshSlot}{}$; emit store from $r$ to $h$
  \State \Return $h$
\EndFunction
\Function{Restore}{$home,r$}
  \State emit load or recipe from $home$ into $r$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

Suppose a register holding the constant 41 is needed for an unrelated
load, and the constant's only future use adds it to a loaded value 8.
Saving and reloading requires a store and a load; the recipe
\texttt{li 41} requires just one instruction on this toy target.
Either path must yield 49. Now replace the definition by a load from
a mutable location that initially holds 41. An intervening write changes
it to 7. Reissuing that load would yield 15 instead of 49; the allocator
must save the original value. Even an immutable load is outside the
recipe catalogue here, since memory instructions may have other
requirements such as fault ordering.

The invariant is that each still-needed evicted value can be recovered
independently of the old register contents. Test after overwriting that
register with a visibly different value, and check stores and reloads
as well as the arithmetic result. A larger catalogue needs a cost
comparison: a multi-instruction recipe may cost more than reloading a
clean spill slot or using a memory operand directly.
